globalThis.BSPOfflineCatalog = {"version":"1.9.0","solver_version":"1.3.0","languages":{"en":{"name":"English","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/","lessons":[{"id":"photon-mass","number":1,"title":"Light, energy and invariant mass","statement":"Photons have mass.","answer":"Light has measurable energy and momentum. A nonzero invariant photon rest mass is a separate hypothesis, not a consequence of radiation pressure or gravity.","context":"Maxwell electrodynamics and the standard photon describe zero invariant mass. A positive invariant mass would change the dispersion relation and can introduce additional polarization and electrostatic screening constraints. Review 6.0 §5.2 treats a positive frequency gap in a candidate sector. Here C and initial energy are normalized to one, so a gap fraction g yields group speed √(1−g²). A photon carrying energy is not proof of a positive gap.","test":"Compare dispersion, static electric forces, polarization and emission/receiver spectra using shared parameters. A bound on rest mass is not a detection.","limitation":"Single fixed-energy dispersion control. Does not derive the gap, its origin or an astronomical drag law.","parameters":{"gap":"Rest-energy fraction"},"metrics":["Group speed / C","Normalized momentum","Energy-equivalent mass / (E/C²)"],"series":["Group speed / C","Momentum C / E"],"xLabel":"Rest-energy fraction","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/photon-mass/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.2; book Chapter 30L","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/photon-mass/","original_statement":"Photons have mass.","equation":"E² = C²p² + Eₘ²;  v_g/C = √(1−g²),  g = Eₘ/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.2,"min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/photon-mass/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/photon-mass/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/photon-mass/"}},{"id":"constant-time","number":2,"title":"One progression, different clock readings","statement":"Time is constant.","answer":"The proposed universal progression T is a research choice. Different measured clock counts must be explained; identical progression does not establish a detectable absolute clock.","context":"Special relativity predicts proper time along an inertial trajectory. Atomic clocks, particle lifetimes and gravitational time comparisons constrain any alternative account. ArcSecs uses T and a process rate ν(T); a candidate must derive each physical rate and reproduce the complete set of comparisons, not just choose a rate that fits one clock.","test":"Compare clock technologies and moving-particle lifetimes using the same specified physical rate law.","limitation":"Constant stipulated rates; the SR comparator is an inertial control, not a derivation of preferred time.","parameters":{"rate":"Reference rate","beta":"Relative speed / c","duration":"Coordinate interval","ratio":"Candidate B/A rate"},"metrics":["Reference counts","Candidate counts","SR counts"],"series":["Reference counts","Candidate counts","SR comparator counts"],"xLabel":"Coordinate interval (s)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/constant-time/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.3; book Chapter 25","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/constant-time/","original_statement":"Time is constant.","equation":"N_A = ν_A T; N_B = ν_B T; N_SR = ν_A T√(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"rate","label":"Reference rate","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":5,"step":0.1,"unit":"Hz"},{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.6,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Coordinate interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"ratio","label":"Candidate B/A rate","default":0.8,"min":0.1,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/constant-time/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/constant-time/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/constant-time/"}},{"id":"light-slowing","number":3,"title":"Distinguishing energy loss from slowing","statement":"Light slows down over time.","answer":"Energy, frequency and group speed are distinct. A fixed-gap drag model permits energy loss with very little speed change, and the zero-gap limit does not slow at all.","context":"Redshifts arise in standard physics from relative motion, gravitational conditions and cosmological evolution. Propagation in media can alter group speed; a spectral shift alone does not identify a mechanism. The fixed-gap momentum-loss control in the book is an explicitly phenomenological branch. Review 6.0 also studies conversion and energy transfer; a stationary loss law alone does not generate observed arrival stretching.","test":"Identify the energy recipient and test pulse stretching, image blur, spectral noise and independent timing.","limitation":"Normalized fixed C, initial E=1, drag imposed rather than derived. No microscopic bath or universal cosmology.","parameters":{"gap":"Rest-energy fraction","drag":"Momentum-loss rate","duration":"Progression interval"},"metrics":["Final energy / initial","Final group speed / C","Transferred energy"],"series":["Energy / initial","Group speed / C","Transferred energy"],"xLabel":"Progression T","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/light-slowing/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 Parts XXXIII–XXXIV; book Chapter 30R","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/light-slowing/","original_statement":"Light slows down over time.","equation":"p = p₀ exp(−γT); E = √(p²+g²); v_g = p/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.1,"min":0,"max":0.9,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"drag","label":"Momentum-loss rate","default":0.3,"min":0,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Progression interval","default":5,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/light-slowing/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/light-slowing/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/light-slowing/"}},{"id":"when","number":4,"title":"Time as event ordering","statement":"Time does not physically exist; it is the “When.”","answer":"The charter treats When as a relation between events. That ontological choice must be distinguished from measured duration and relativistic proper time.","context":"In relativity, proper time is measurable along a worldline; coordinate times describe events in chosen frames. Neither requires a flowing material. The physical-first account models ordered source events, transport and receiver counts separately. A variable transit delay can change arrival intervals, but an identical delay cannot.","test":"Measure a series of pulses with calibrated endpoint clocks; vary transit conditions independently.","limitation":"Stationary endpoints, constant transit delay, identical clock units; not a synchronization theory or a full redshift model.","parameters":{"period":"Emission interval","delay":"Fixed transit delay"},"metrics":["Emission interval","Arrival interval","First arrival"],"series":["Emission time","Arrival time"],"xLabel":"Pulse index","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/when/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/when/","original_statement":"Time does not physically exist; it is the “When.”","equation":"t_emit(n)=nP; t_arr(n)=nP+L/C; Δt_arr=P","parameter_schema":[{"key":"period","label":"Emission interval","default":2,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"delay","label":"Fixed transit delay","default":5,"min":0,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/when/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/when/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/when/"}},{"id":"where","number":5,"title":"Space as measurable relations","statement":"Space does not physically exist; it is the “Where.”","answer":"Relative position, separation and geometry are measurable. The choice not to call space a material does not remove geometric laws.","context":"Euclidean geometry is a useful local approximation; relativistic geometry predicts measured trajectories and timing in its domain. Coordinates themselves are not material objects. ArcSecs treats Where as relative arrangement. Physical states and interactions must give coordinate-independent predictions for actual rulers and other instruments.","test":"Repeat ranging with different reference origins and compare invariant separations in the declared measurement regime.","limitation":"Flat two-dimensional geometry; not a model of curved geometry, cosmological distance or absolute coordinates.","parameters":{"dx":"Horizontal separation","dy":"Vertical separation","shift":"Common origin offset"},"metrics":["Object A x","Object B x","Invariant separation"],"series":["Object A x","Object B x","Separation"],"xLabel":"Common offset","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/where/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1; book foundations interpretation","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/where/","original_statement":"Space does not physically exist; it is the “Where.”","equation":"d = √((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"dx","label":"Horizontal separation","default":3,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"dy","label":"Vertical separation","default":4,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"shift","label":"Common origin offset","default":2,"min":-10,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/where/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/where/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/where/"}},{"id":"physical-existence","number":6,"title":"Physical attributes and system mass","statement":"All that physically exists at any moment occupies area and has mass at that moment.","answer":"Physical states carry measurable energy and momentum, but energy-equivalent mass, invariant mass and geometric extent are distinct quantities.","context":"For a system, invariant mass is defined by total four-momentum. A field state need not have a rigid classical boundary or nonzero individual rest mass. The charter asks for a physical inventory. Its technical interpretation requires defining mass and extent operationally before proposing an invariant mass for every entity. This control uses the standard massless two-photon reference.","test":"Measure total energy and vector momentum of a closed system; distinguish the aggregate invariant from constituent invariant masses.","limitation":"Two massless packets of equal energy; no localization measurement or proof that every entity has a sharp size.","parameters":{"energy":"Equal photon energy","angle":"Angle between momenta"},"metrics":["System rest energy Mc²","Total energy","Individual photon invariant mass"],"series":["System rest energy Mc²"],"xLabel":"Angle (degrees)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/physical-existence/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2 and §5.2","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/physical-existence/","original_statement":"All that physically exists at any moment occupies area and has mass at that moment.","equation":"M²c⁴ = 2E₁E₂(1−cos θ)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"energy","label":"Equal photon energy","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"angle","label":"Angle between momenta","default":180,"min":0,"max":180,"step":1,"unit":"deg"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/physical-existence/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/physical-existence/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/physical-existence/"}},{"id":"spacetime","number":7,"title":"Geometry versus a literal fabric","statement":"Spacetime does not physically exist.","answer":"Relativistic geometry is a successful predictive framework. Rejecting a literal fabric metaphor does not reject measured proper times, trajectories or gravity.","context":"The standard inertial proper-time factor is √(1−β²). General relativity extends the predictions to gravity using a dynamical metric. The research seeks physical interaction laws that recover the same predictions and offer independently discriminating tests. The standard factor here is a comparator, not a proposed alternative mechanism.","test":"Compare precision clock measurements, orbital effects, gravitational waves and light deflection with a derived alternative law.","limitation":"Inertial special-relativity reference only. No gravitational metric calculation or replacement theory is supplied.","parameters":{"beta":"Relative speed / c","duration":"Reference interval"},"metrics":["Proper interval","Reference interval","Proper/reference interval"],"series":["Proper interval (s)","Coordinate interval (s)"],"xLabel":"Relative speed / c","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/spacetime/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/spacetime/","original_statement":"Spacetime does not physically exist.","equation":"Δτ/Δt = √(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.8,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Reference interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/spacetime/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/spacetime/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/spacetime/"}},{"id":"fastest-signal","number":8,"title":"Group speed and causal speed","statement":"Light is not the fastest thing in the universe.","answer":"A field characteristic may be faster than a slow massive packet without exceeding the local causal maximum. No controllable faster-than-light signal is demonstrated.","context":"In standard vacuum relativity c is the local causal speed. Group speed and front speed in dispersive media are distinct. A closing rate is not a local information speed. C-01 separates the emission ceiling, electromagnetic front, all-sector causal characteristic, packet group speed and separation rate. The research requires a derived ordering and physical signal protocol.","test":"Test actual signal fronts with independently synchronized endpoints and an explicitly stated causal theory.","limitation":"Normalized stationary paths; the front is a specified reference, not an experimentally detected new faster field.","parameters":{"fraction":"Packet speed / C","distance":"Path length / C"},"metrics":["Packet arrival","Reference arrival","Arrival difference"],"series":["Packet arrival time","Reference arrival time"],"xLabel":"Distance / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/fastest-signal/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3, C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/fastest-signal/","original_statement":"Light is not the fastest thing in the universe.","equation":"t_packet = D/v_g; t_front = D/C; Δt = D(1/v_g−1/C)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"fraction","label":"Packet speed / C","default":0.7,"min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"distance","label":"Path length / C","default":10,"min":0.1,"max":30,"step":0.1,"unit":"time units"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/fastest-signal/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/fastest-signal/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/fastest-signal/"}},{"id":"separation","number":9,"title":"Closing and separation rates versus causality","statement":"Things move apart from each other faster than causality; time, space, or spacetime cannot be physically stretched because they are not physical substances to stretch.","answer":"Coordinate separation rates may exceed a local light-speed benchmark; this does not imply that one body or signal travels locally faster than c.","context":"Special relativity gives relative speed 2β/(1+β²) times c for opposite equal speeds in one inertial frame. Cosmological recession is a distinct large-scale quantity. Review 6.0 C-01 uses this two-spacecraft thought experiment to separate inherited motion and emission. It expressly does not demonstrate single-photon superluminal motion.","test":"Measure signal arrival and relative velocity with the appropriate clocks and frame transformations.","limitation":"Equal opposite inertial velocities, not a cosmological metric or a superluminal communication mechanism.","parameters":{"beta":"Each ship speed / C"},"metrics":["Coordinate separation rate / C","SR relative speed / C","Local causal benchmark / C"],"series":["Common-frame separation rate / C","SR measured relative speed / C","Local benchmark"],"xLabel":"Each ship speed / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/separation/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3; C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/separation/","original_statement":"Things move apart from each other faster than causality; time, space, or spacetime cannot be physically stretched because they are not physical substances to stretch.","equation":"V_sep/C = 2β; v_rel/C = 2β/(1+β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Each ship speed / C","default":0.9,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/separation/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/separation/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/separation/"}},{"id":"cosmic-size","number":10,"title":"Observable reach and total cosmic extent","statement":"The size of the universe is unknown.","answer":"A finite causal reach does not determine the total physical extent of the universe. The observable horizon is not necessarily a material wall.","context":"Cosmological particle horizons depend on the expansion history and differ from Hubble radius and event horizon. Observations constrain curvature and accessible structure, not every possible parent domain. ArcSecs considers the total cosmos separately from the observed domain. A real boundary or outside region would require a field/interface model and observational signature.","test":"Use independent observations of curvature, topology, sky signatures and causal histories; a domain boundary needs separate evidence.","limitation":"Stationary Euclidean centered sphere with prescribed reach; not a calculation of the actual observable universe or its size.","parameters":{"reach":"Signal reach","domain":"Assumed domain radius"},"metrics":["Signal reach","Assumed domain radius","Accessible fraction"],"series":["Accessible volume fraction"],"xLabel":"Assumed spherical radius","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-size/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and Chapters 30–32","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-size/","original_statement":"The size of the universe is unknown.","equation":"R_reach = C T; observed fraction = min(1,(R_reach/R_domain)³)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"reach","label":"Signal reach","default":4,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"domain","label":"Assumed domain radius","default":8,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-size/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-size/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-size/"}},{"id":"cosmic-age","number":11,"title":"Modeled cosmic age and total history","statement":"The age of the universe is unknown.","answer":"The conventional cosmic age is an inference from a calibrated expansion history. The duration of all hypothetical physical reality is a different, unanswered question.","context":"A flat matter–Λ reference gives a calculable expansion age for specified H₀ and matter fraction. The full standard inference includes radiation, observational constraints and uncertainties. The research distinguishes an age inferred for our domain from an unknown parent history. It retains the hot Big Bang as a possible physical history and requires independent chronometers.","test":"Compare cosmic expansion fits with stellar and other physical age indicators, using the same model assumptions and uncertainties.","limitation":"Flat matter–Λ reference, radiation omitted for this analytic lesson; no data fit or inference about all reality.","parameters":{"h0":"Hubble constant","matter":"Matter fraction"},"metrics":["Modeled age","Matter fraction","Λ fraction"],"series":["Model age (Gyr)"],"xLabel":"Hubble constant (km/s/Mpc)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-age/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-age/","original_statement":"The age of the universe is unknown.","equation":"t₀ = 2 asinh(√((1−Ωm)/Ωm))/(3 H₀√(1−Ωm))","parameter_schema":[{"key":"h0","label":"Hubble constant","default":70,"min":45,"max":95,"step":1,"unit":"km/s/Mpc"},{"key":"matter","label":"Matter fraction","default":0.3,"min":0.05,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-age/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-age/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-age/"}},{"id":"distance-inference","number":12,"title":"When astronomical distances and sizes can change","statement":"Objects in the universe are smaller and closer than they appear.","answer":"Distance corrections are testable and can have either sign. The charter’s smaller-and-closer premise must not become an assumed correction for every object.","context":"Geometric anchors, calibrated candles, rulers, spectroscopy and time intervals give complementary constraints. A reclassified foreground object does not invalidate the distances of spectroscopically confirmed galaxies. ArcSecs calls for geometric-first audits and joint fits, with positive and negative controls. This lesson isolates an unmodeled extinction term and a calibrated distance.","test":"Compare geometric anchors, extinction, flux, angles, spectra and arrival intervals with shared parameters and held-out observations.","limitation":"Signed magnitude offset is a sensitivity control; negative values model calibration/brightening bias, not physical negative dust extinction. No universal correction or astronomical fit.","parameters":{"actual":"Calibrated distance","extinction":"Unmodeled attenuation"},"metrics":["Inferred distance","Calibrated distance","Inferred/calibrated size"],"series":["Inferred distance (Mpc)","Calibrated distance (Mpc)"],"xLabel":"Signed magnitude bias","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/distance-inference/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and distance audit","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/distance-inference/","original_statement":"Objects in the universe are smaller and closer than they appear.","equation":"D_inferred/D = 10^(A/5); size ratio = distance ratio (fixed small angle)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"actual","label":"Calibrated distance","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"Mpc"},{"key":"extinction","label":"Unmodeled attenuation","default":0.5,"min":-1,"max":3,"step":0.05,"unit":"mag"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/distance-inference/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/distance-inference/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/distance-inference/"}}],"glossary":[{"term":"Invariant mass","definition":"Mass defined by the total energy–momentum invariant, not simply energy divided by c².","lesson":"photon-mass"},{"term":"Group speed","definition":"Rate of propagation of a wave-packet envelope in a specified dispersion law. Distinct from signal-front speed.","lesson":"light-slowing"},{"term":"Proper time","definition":"Time measured by an ideal clock along its worldline in relativity.","lesson":"constant-time"},{"term":"Universal progression T","definition":"Proposed common evolution parameter; not an experimentally established absolute clock.","lesson":"when"},{"term":"Causal front","definition":"Leading boundary of a causal disturbance in a specified theory, not necessarily the group speed.","lesson":"fastest-signal"},{"term":"Separation rate","definition":"Rate of change of distance under a specified coordinate convention; not automatically a local relative speed.","lesson":"separation"},{"term":"Observable horizon","definition":"Limit of causal accessibility determined by the assumed cosmic history, not necessarily a physical wall.","lesson":"cosmic-size"},{"term":"Hubble constant","definition":"Present expansion-rate parameter in a conventional cosmological model.","lesson":"cosmic-age"},{"term":"Distance bias","definition":"Difference between an inferred and calibrated distance under declared assumptions. It can have either sign.","lesson":"distance-inference"},{"term":"Negative control","definition":"Specified model condition expected to remove an effect or reveal a failure; passing it is not empirical validation.","lesson":"spacetime"}]},"es":{"name":"Español","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/","lessons":[{"id":"photon-mass","number":1,"title":"Luz, energía y masa invariante","statement":"Los fotones tienen masa.","answer":"La luz transporta energía y momento medibles. Una masa invariante no nula del fotón es una hipótesis distinta, no una consecuencia de la presión de radiación ni de la gravedad.","context":"La electrodinámica estándar describe fotones sin masa invariante. El modelo con brecha de energía en Review 6.0 §5.2 propone una relación de dispersión diferente; no deriva el origen de la brecha.","test":"Comparar dispersión, fuerzas electrostáticas, polarización y espectros de emisión y recepción con parámetros compartidos. Una cota de masa no es una detección.","limitation":"Control de dispersión a energía fija; no deriva una ley de arrastre astronómico.","parameters":{"gap":"Fracción de energía en reposo"},"metrics":["Velocidad de grupo / C","Momento normalizado","Masa equivalente de energía / (E/C²)"],"series":["Velocidad de grupo / C","Momento C / E"],"xLabel":"Fracción de energía en reposo","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/photon-mass/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.2; book Chapter 30L","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/photon-mass/","original_statement":"Photons have mass.","equation":"E² = C²p² + Eₘ²;  v_g/C = √(1−g²),  g = Eₘ/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.2,"min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/photon-mass/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/photon-mass/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/photon-mass/"}},{"id":"constant-time","number":2,"title":"Una progresión, distintas lecturas de reloj","statement":"El tiempo es constante.","answer":"La progresión universal T es una elección de modelo. Las diferencias observadas entre relojes requieren una explicación; no establecen un reloj absoluto detectable.","context":"La relatividad especial predice el tiempo propio. ArcSecs propone tasas de procesos ν(T); deben derivarse y reproducir todas las comparaciones, no ajustarse a un solo reloj.","test":"Comparar tecnologías de reloj y vidas medias de partículas en movimiento mediante una misma ley física de tasas.","limitation":"Tasas constantes estipuladas; la referencia relativista es inercial y no deriva un tiempo preferido.","parameters":{"rate":"Frecuencia de referencia","beta":"Velocidad relativa / c","duration":"Intervalo de coordenadas","ratio":"Razón de tasas B/A"},"metrics":["Conteos de referencia","Conteos del candidato","Conteos relativistas"],"series":["Conteos de referencia","Conteos del candidato","Conteos relativistas"],"xLabel":"Intervalo de coordenadas (s)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/constant-time/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.3; book Chapter 25","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/constant-time/","original_statement":"Time is constant.","equation":"N_A = ν_A T; N_B = ν_B T; N_SR = ν_A T√(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"rate","label":"Reference rate","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":5,"step":0.1,"unit":"Hz"},{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.6,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Coordinate interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"ratio","label":"Candidate B/A rate","default":0.8,"min":0.1,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/constant-time/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/constant-time/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/constant-time/"}},{"id":"light-slowing","number":3,"title":"Energía y velocidad durante la propagación","statement":"La luz se ralentiza con el tiempo.","answer":"Energía, frecuencia y velocidad de grupo son distintas. En el modelo de arrastre con brecha fija puede perderse energía con un cambio de velocidad muy pequeño; sin brecha no disminuye la velocidad.","context":"El corrimiento al rojo estándar puede deberse al movimiento, la gravedad o la expansión. El arrastre propuesto es fenomenológico; por sí solo no reproduce el estiramiento de los intervalos de llegada.","test":"Identificar el receptor de energía y medir estiramiento de pulsos, desenfoque, ruido espectral y tiempos independientes.","limitation":"C fija y energía inicial unitaria; arrastre impuesto, no derivado. No hay un medio microscópico ni una cosmología universal.","parameters":{"gap":"Fracción de energía en reposo","drag":"Tasa de pérdida de momento","duration":"Intervalo de progresión"},"metrics":["Energía final / inicial","Velocidad de grupo final / C","Energía transferida"],"series":["Energía / inicial","Velocidad de grupo / C","Energía transferida"],"xLabel":"Progresión T","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/light-slowing/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 Parts XXXIII–XXXIV; book Chapter 30R","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/light-slowing/","original_statement":"Light slows down over time.","equation":"p = p₀ exp(−γT); E = √(p²+g²); v_g = p/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.1,"min":0,"max":0.9,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"drag","label":"Momentum-loss rate","default":0.3,"min":0,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Progression interval","default":5,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/light-slowing/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/light-slowing/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/light-slowing/"}},{"id":"when","number":4,"title":"El cuándo: eventos y llegada de pulsos","statement":"El tiempo no existe físicamente; es el «cuándo».","answer":"La carta considera el cuándo una relación entre eventos. Esta elección ontológica no equivale a negar las duraciones medidas ni el tiempo propio relativista.","context":"La relatividad distingue tiempo propio y coordenadas temporales. El enfoque físico separa emisión, transporte y recepción: un retardo constante no modifica los intervalos de llegada.","test":"Medir una serie de pulsos con relojes calibrados en los extremos y variar las condiciones de tránsito independientemente.","limitation":"Extremos estacionarios, retardo fijo y unidades de reloj idénticas; no constituye una teoría de sincronización ni del corrimiento al rojo.","parameters":{"period":"Intervalo de emisión","delay":"Retardo fijo de tránsito"},"metrics":["Intervalo de emisión","Intervalo de llegada","Primera llegada"],"series":["Tiempo de emisión","Tiempo de llegada"],"xLabel":"Índice del pulso","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/when/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/when/","original_statement":"Time does not physically exist; it is the “When.”","equation":"t_emit(n)=nP; t_arr(n)=nP+L/C; Δt_arr=P","parameter_schema":[{"key":"period","label":"Emission interval","default":2,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"delay","label":"Fixed transit delay","default":5,"min":0,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/when/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/when/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/when/"}},{"id":"where","number":5,"title":"El dónde: posición relativa y geometría","statement":"El espacio no existe físicamente; es el «dónde».","answer":"La posición relativa, la separación y la geometría son medibles. No considerar el espacio una sustancia no elimina las leyes geométricas.","context":"La geometría euclidiana aproxima situaciones locales; la geometría relativista predice trayectorias y tiempos. ArcSecs exige que las interacciones den predicciones independientes del origen de coordenadas.","test":"Repetir mediciones de distancia usando diferentes orígenes y comparar separaciones invariantes en el régimen declarado.","limitation":"Geometría plana bidimensional; no modela curvatura, distancias cosmológicas ni coordenadas absolutas.","parameters":{"dx":"Separación horizontal","dy":"Separación vertical","shift":"Desplazamiento común del origen"},"metrics":["Coordenada x de A","Coordenada x de B","Separación invariante"],"series":["Coordenada x de A","Coordenada x de B","Separación"],"xLabel":"Desplazamiento común","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/where/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1; book foundations interpretation","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/where/","original_statement":"Space does not physically exist; it is the “Where.”","equation":"d = √((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"dx","label":"Horizontal separation","default":3,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"dy","label":"Vertical separation","default":4,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"shift","label":"Common origin offset","default":2,"min":-10,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/where/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/where/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/where/"}},{"id":"physical-existence","number":6,"title":"Existencia física y masa de un sistema","statement":"Todo lo que existe físicamente en un momento dado ocupa área y tiene masa en ese momento.","answer":"La energía y el momento son medibles. La masa equivalente de energía, la masa invariante y la extensión geométrica son conceptos diferentes.","context":"La masa invariante de un sistema se define por su cuadrimomento total. Este control usa dos fotones estándar sin masa; su sistema puede tener masa invariante positiva sin que cada fotón la tenga.","test":"Medir energía total y momento vectorial de un sistema cerrado; distinguir el invariante agregado de los invariantes individuales.","limitation":"Dos paquetes sin masa de igual energía; no prueba que cada entidad tenga un tamaño definido.","parameters":{"energy":"Energía igual de cada fotón","angle":"Ángulo entre momentos"},"metrics":["Energía en reposo del sistema Mc²","Energía total","Masa invariante de cada fotón"],"series":["Energía en reposo del sistema Mc²"],"xLabel":"Ángulo (grados)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/physical-existence/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2 and §5.2","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/physical-existence/","original_statement":"All that physically exists at any moment occupies area and has mass at that moment.","equation":"M²c⁴ = 2E₁E₂(1−cos θ)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"energy","label":"Equal photon energy","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"angle","label":"Angle between momenta","default":180,"min":0,"max":180,"step":1,"unit":"deg"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/physical-existence/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/physical-existence/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/physical-existence/"}},{"id":"spacetime","number":7,"title":"Geometría y sustancia física","statement":"El espacio-tiempo no existe físicamente.","answer":"La geometría relativista tiene éxito predictivo. Rechazar una metáfora de tejido literal no invalida los tiempos propios, las trayectorias o la gravedad medidos.","context":"La relatividad especial predice √(1−β²) como factor inercial de tiempo propio. La relatividad general emplea una métrica dinámica. El programa busca leyes de interacción que recuperen esas predicciones y permitan pruebas discriminantes.","test":"Comparar relojes de precisión, órbitas, ondas gravitacionales y desviación de la luz con una ley alternativa derivada.","limitation":"Referencia inercial de relatividad especial; sin cálculo métrico gravitatorio ni teoría sustitutiva.","parameters":{"beta":"Velocidad relativa / c","duration":"Intervalo de referencia"},"metrics":["Intervalo propio","Intervalo de referencia","Razón propio/referencia"],"series":["Intervalo propio (s)","Intervalo de coordenadas (s)"],"xLabel":"Velocidad relativa / c","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/spacetime/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/spacetime/","original_statement":"Spacetime does not physically exist.","equation":"Δτ/Δt = √(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.8,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Reference interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/spacetime/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/spacetime/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/spacetime/"}},{"id":"fastest-signal","number":8,"title":"Velocidad de grupo y causalidad","statement":"La luz no es lo más rápido del universo.","answer":"Una característica de campo puede superar la velocidad de un paquete lento sin superar el límite causal local. No se demuestra ninguna señal controlable más rápida que la luz.","context":"En la relatividad estándar en el vacío, c es la velocidad causal local. El proyecto C-01 distingue techo de emisión, frente electromagnético, características causales, velocidad de grupo y tasa de separación.","test":"Medir frentes de señal con extremos sincronizados de forma independiente y una teoría causal explícita.","limitation":"Trayectos estacionarios normalizados; el frente es una referencia estipulada, no un campo más rápido detectado.","parameters":{"fraction":"Velocidad del paquete / C","distance":"Longitud del trayecto / C"},"metrics":["Llegada del paquete","Llegada de referencia","Diferencia de llegada"],"series":["Tiempo de llegada del paquete","Tiempo de llegada de referencia"],"xLabel":"Distancia / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/fastest-signal/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3, C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/fastest-signal/","original_statement":"Light is not the fastest thing in the universe.","equation":"t_packet = D/v_g; t_front = D/C; Δt = D(1/v_g−1/C)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"fraction","label":"Packet speed / C","default":0.7,"min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"distance","label":"Path length / C","default":10,"min":0.1,"max":30,"step":0.1,"unit":"time units"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/fastest-signal/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/fastest-signal/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/fastest-signal/"}},{"id":"separation","number":9,"title":"Separación de objetos y velocidad local","statement":"Las cosas se alejan entre sí más rápido que la causalidad; el tiempo, el espacio o el espacio-tiempo no pueden estirarse físicamente porque no son sustancias físicas que puedan estirarse.","answer":"Una tasa de separación en coordenadas puede superar c sin que un cuerpo o una señal lo haga localmente.","context":"Para velocidades opuestas iguales βc en un marco inercial, la velocidad relativa relativista es 2βc/(1+β²). El ejemplo de dos naves de C-01 distingue la separación de la propagación de una señal.","test":"Medir llegada de señales y velocidad relativa con relojes adecuados y transformaciones de marco.","limitation":"Velocidades inerciales iguales y opuestas; no es una métrica cosmológica ni un mecanismo de comunicación superlumínica.","parameters":{"beta":"Velocidad de cada nave / C"},"metrics":["Tasa de separación en coordenadas / C","Velocidad relativa relativista / C","Referencia causal local / C"],"series":["Separación en marco común / C","Velocidad relativa relativista / C","Referencia local"],"xLabel":"Velocidad de cada nave / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/separation/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3; C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/separation/","original_statement":"Things move apart from each other faster than causality; time, space, or spacetime cannot be physically stretched because they are not physical substances to stretch.","equation":"V_sep/C = 2β; v_rel/C = 2β/(1+β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Each ship speed / C","default":0.9,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/separation/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/separation/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/separation/"}},{"id":"cosmic-size","number":10,"title":"Horizonte y extensión del cosmos","statement":"Se desconoce el tamaño del universo.","answer":"Un alcance causal finito no determina la extensión total. El horizonte observable no es necesariamente una pared material.","context":"Los horizontes cosmológicos dependen de la expansión y no son idénticos al radio de Hubble. ArcSecs distingue el dominio observado de un cosmos total hipotético; una frontera real requiere su propio mecanismo y señal observacional.","test":"Contrastar curvatura, topología, firmas del cielo e historias causales independientes.","limitation":"Esfera euclidiana centrada y estática con alcance impuesto; no calcula el universo observable real.","parameters":{"reach":"Alcance de señal","domain":"Radio de dominio supuesto"},"metrics":["Alcance de señal","Radio de dominio supuesto","Fracción accesible"],"series":["Fracción de volumen accesible"],"xLabel":"Radio esférico supuesto","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-size/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and Chapters 30–32","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-size/","original_statement":"The size of the universe is unknown.","equation":"R_reach = C T; observed fraction = min(1,(R_reach/R_domain)³)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"reach","label":"Signal reach","default":4,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"domain","label":"Assumed domain radius","default":8,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-size/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-size/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-size/"}},{"id":"cosmic-age","number":11,"title":"Edad cosmológica e historia total","statement":"Se desconoce la edad del universo.","answer":"La edad cosmológica convencional se infiere de una historia de expansión calibrada. La duración de toda realidad física hipotética es otra cuestión.","context":"Una referencia plana de materia y Λ permite calcular una edad con H₀ y fracción de materia. ArcSecs conserva el Big Bang caliente como historia posible y distingue la historia del dominio de una historia anterior desconocida.","test":"Comparar ajustes de expansión con edades estelares y otros cronómetros independientes, incluyendo incertidumbres.","limitation":"Referencia plana de materia y Λ sin radiación en esta lección analítica; sin ajuste de datos ni inferencia sobre toda realidad.","parameters":{"h0":"Constante de Hubble","matter":"Fracción de materia"},"metrics":["Edad del modelo","Fracción de materia","Fracción de Λ"],"series":["Edad del modelo (Gyr)"],"xLabel":"Constante de Hubble (km/s/Mpc)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-age/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-age/","original_statement":"The age of the universe is unknown.","equation":"t₀ = 2 asinh(√((1−Ωm)/Ωm))/(3 H₀√(1−Ωm))","parameter_schema":[{"key":"h0","label":"Hubble constant","default":70,"min":45,"max":95,"step":1,"unit":"km/s/Mpc"},{"key":"matter","label":"Matter fraction","default":0.3,"min":0.05,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-age/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-age/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-age/"}},{"id":"distance-inference","number":12,"title":"Distancias y tamaños astronómicos","statement":"Los objetos del universo son más pequeños y están más cerca de lo que parecen.","answer":"Las correcciones de distancia pueden tener ambos signos. La premisa de objetos más pequeños y cercanos no es una corrección universal establecida.","context":"Anclas geométricas, luminosidades calibradas, reglas, espectros e intervalos temporales aportan restricciones complementarias. Este control aísla un sesgo de magnitud respecto a una distancia calibrada.","test":"Contrastar anclas geométricas, extinción, flujo, ángulos, espectros e intervalos con parámetros comunes y datos reservados.","limitation":"Sesgo de magnitud con signo; los valores negativos representan brillo o calibración, no polvo con extinción física negativa. Sin ajuste astronómico.","parameters":{"actual":"Distancia calibrada","extinction":"Atenuación no modelada"},"metrics":["Distancia inferida","Distancia calibrada","Tamaño inferido/calibrado"],"series":["Distancia inferida (Mpc)","Distancia calibrada (Mpc)"],"xLabel":"Sesgo de magnitud con signo","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/distance-inference/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and distance audit","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/distance-inference/","original_statement":"Objects in the universe are smaller and closer than they appear.","equation":"D_inferred/D = 10^(A/5); size ratio = distance ratio (fixed small angle)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"actual","label":"Calibrated distance","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"Mpc"},{"key":"extinction","label":"Unmodeled attenuation","default":0.5,"min":-1,"max":3,"step":0.05,"unit":"mag"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/distance-inference/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/distance-inference/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/distance-inference/"}}],"glossary":[{"term":"Masa invariante","definition":"Masa definida por el invariante de energía y momento totales, no simplemente energía dividida por c².","lesson":"photon-mass"},{"term":"Velocidad de grupo","definition":"Velocidad de propagación de la envolvente de un paquete de ondas en una relación de dispersión dada. Distinta de la velocidad del frente.","lesson":"light-slowing"},{"term":"Tiempo propio","definition":"Tiempo medido por un reloj ideal a lo largo de su línea de universo en relatividad.","lesson":"constant-time"},{"term":"Progresión universal T","definition":"Parámetro común de evolución propuesto; no es un reloj absoluto establecido experimentalmente.","lesson":"when"},{"term":"Frente causal","definition":"Límite delantero de una perturbación causal en una teoría dada, no necesariamente la velocidad de grupo.","lesson":"fastest-signal"},{"term":"Tasa de separación","definition":"Variación de distancia por unidad de tiempo según una convención de coordenadas; no equivale necesariamente a una velocidad relativa local.","lesson":"separation"},{"term":"Horizonte observable","definition":"Límite de accesibilidad causal determinado por la historia cósmica supuesta, no necesariamente una pared física.","lesson":"cosmic-size"},{"term":"Constante de Hubble","definition":"Parámetro de la tasa de expansión actual en un modelo cosmológico convencional.","lesson":"cosmic-age"},{"term":"Sesgo de distancia","definition":"Diferencia entre una distancia inferida y otra calibrada bajo supuestos declarados. Puede tener ambos signos.","lesson":"distance-inference"},{"term":"Control negativo","definition":"Condición especificada del modelo que debe eliminar un efecto o revelar un fallo; superarlo no es una validación empírica.","lesson":"spacetime"}]},"fr":{"name":"Français","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/","lessons":[{"id":"photon-mass","number":1,"title":"Lumière, énergie et masse invariante","statement":"Les photons ont une masse.","answer":"La lumière transporte une énergie et une quantité de mouvement mesurables. Une masse invariante non nulle du photon constitue une hypothèse distincte, non une conséquence de la pression de radiation ou de la gravité.","context":"L’électrodynamique standard décrit des photons de masse invariante nulle. Le modèle à seuil énergétique de Review 6.0 §5.2 modifie la dispersion sans dériver l’origine du seuil.","test":"Comparer dispersion, forces électrostatiques, polarisation et spectres d’émission et de réception avec des paramètres communs. Une borne de masse n’est pas une détection.","limitation":"Contrôle de dispersion à énergie fixe ; ne dérive ni le seuil ni une loi de freinage astronomique.","parameters":{"gap":"Fraction d’énergie au repos"},"metrics":["Vitesse de groupe / C","Quantité de mouvement normalisée","Masse équivalente énergétique / (E/C²)"],"series":["Vitesse de groupe / C","Quantité de mouvement C / E"],"xLabel":"Fraction d’énergie au repos","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/photon-mass/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.2; book Chapter 30L","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/photon-mass/","original_statement":"Photons have mass.","equation":"E² = C²p² + Eₘ²;  v_g/C = √(1−g²),  g = Eₘ/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.2,"min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/photon-mass/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/photon-mass/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/photon-mass/"}},{"id":"constant-time","number":2,"title":"Une progression, des horloges différentes","statement":"Le temps est constant.","answer":"La progression universelle T est un choix de modèle. Les différences de comptage des horloges demandent une explication, mais ne prouvent pas une horloge absolue détectable.","context":"La relativité restreinte prédit le temps propre. ArcSecs propose des taux de processus ν(T) ; il faut les dériver et reproduire toutes les comparaisons, pas seulement ajuster une horloge.","test":"Comparer différentes technologies d’horloge et durées de vie de particules en mouvement avec une même loi physique.","limitation":"Taux constants stipulés ; le comparateur relativiste inertiel ne dérive pas un temps privilégié.","parameters":{"rate":"Fréquence de référence","beta":"Vitesse relative / c","duration":"Intervalle de coordonnées","ratio":"Rapport des taux B/A"},"metrics":["Comptage de référence","Comptage candidat","Comptage relativiste"],"series":["Comptage de référence","Comptage candidat","Comptage relativiste"],"xLabel":"Intervalle de coordonnées (s)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/constant-time/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.3; book Chapter 25","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/constant-time/","original_statement":"Time is constant.","equation":"N_A = ν_A T; N_B = ν_B T; N_SR = ν_A T√(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"rate","label":"Reference rate","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":5,"step":0.1,"unit":"Hz"},{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.6,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Coordinate interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"ratio","label":"Candidate B/A rate","default":0.8,"min":0.1,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/constant-time/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/constant-time/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/constant-time/"}},{"id":"light-slowing","number":3,"title":"Énergie et vitesse pendant la propagation","statement":"La lumière ralentit au cours du temps.","answer":"Énergie, fréquence et vitesse de groupe sont distinctes. Un freinage à seuil fixe peut faire perdre de l’énergie avec très peu de variation de vitesse ; sans seuil, la vitesse reste constante.","context":"Les décalages spectraux standards proviennent notamment du mouvement, de la gravité et de l’expansion. Le freinage proposé est phénoménologique et ne suffit pas à reproduire l’étirement des intervalles d’arrivée.","test":"Identifier le récepteur de l’énergie et tester étirement des impulsions, flou, bruit spectral et chronométrage indépendant.","limitation":"C fixe et énergie initiale unitaire ; freinage imposé, non dérivé. Aucun milieu microscopique ni cosmologie universelle.","parameters":{"gap":"Fraction d’énergie au repos","drag":"Taux de perte de quantité de mouvement","duration":"Intervalle de progression"},"metrics":["Énergie finale / initiale","Vitesse de groupe finale / C","Énergie transférée"],"series":["Énergie / initiale","Vitesse de groupe / C","Énergie transférée"],"xLabel":"Progression T","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/light-slowing/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 Parts XXXIII–XXXIV; book Chapter 30R","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/light-slowing/","original_statement":"Light slows down over time.","equation":"p = p₀ exp(−γT); E = √(p²+g²); v_g = p/E","parameter_schema":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.1,"min":0,"max":0.9,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"drag","label":"Momentum-loss rate","default":0.3,"min":0,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Progression interval","default":5,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/light-slowing/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/light-slowing/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/light-slowing/"}},{"id":"when","number":4,"title":"Le quand : événements et arrivée des impulsions","statement":"Le temps n’existe pas physiquement ; il est le « quand ».","answer":"La charte traite le quand comme une relation entre événements. Ce choix ontologique se distingue des durées mesurées et du temps propre relativiste.","context":"La relativité distingue temps propre et coordonnées temporelles. L’approche physique sépare émission, transport et réception : un retard constant ne change pas les intervalles d’arrivée.","test":"Mesurer des impulsions avec des horloges calibrées aux extrémités et faire varier indépendamment le transport.","limitation":"Extrémités immobiles, retard fixe, unités d’horloge identiques ; ni théorie de synchronisation ni modèle complet du décalage spectral.","parameters":{"period":"Intervalle d’émission","delay":"Retard de transit fixe"},"metrics":["Intervalle d’émission","Intervalle d’arrivée","Première arrivée"],"series":["Instant d’émission","Instant d’arrivée"],"xLabel":"Indice de l’impulsion","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/when/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/when/","original_statement":"Time does not physically exist; it is the “When.”","equation":"t_emit(n)=nP; t_arr(n)=nP+L/C; Δt_arr=P","parameter_schema":[{"key":"period","label":"Emission interval","default":2,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"delay","label":"Fixed transit delay","default":5,"min":0,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/when/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/when/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/when/"}},{"id":"where","number":5,"title":"Le où : position relative et géométrie","statement":"L’espace n’existe pas physiquement ; il est le « où ».","answer":"Position relative, séparation et géométrie sont mesurables. Ne pas considérer l’espace comme une substance ne supprime pas les lois géométriques.","context":"La géométrie euclidienne est une approximation locale ; la géométrie relativiste prédit les trajectoires et les durées. ArcSecs exige des prédictions indépendantes du choix de l’origine.","test":"Répéter les mesures de distance avec différentes origines et comparer les séparations invariantes dans le régime déclaré.","limitation":"Géométrie plane à deux dimensions ; ni courbure, ni distance cosmologique, ni coordonnées absolues.","parameters":{"dx":"Séparation horizontale","dy":"Séparation verticale","shift":"Décalage commun de l’origine"},"metrics":["Coordonnée x de A","Coordonnée x de B","Séparation invariante"],"series":["Coordonnée x de A","Coordonnée x de B","Séparation"],"xLabel":"Décalage commun","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/where/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1; book foundations interpretation","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/where/","original_statement":"Space does not physically exist; it is the “Where.”","equation":"d = √((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"dx","label":"Horizontal separation","default":3,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"dy","label":"Vertical separation","default":4,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"shift","label":"Common origin offset","default":2,"min":-10,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/where/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/where/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/where/"}},{"id":"physical-existence","number":6,"title":"Existence physique et masse d’un système","statement":"Tout ce qui existe physiquement à un instant donné occupe une aire et possède une masse à cet instant.","answer":"Énergie et quantité de mouvement sont mesurables. Masse équivalente énergétique, masse invariante et extension géométrique sont des notions distinctes.","context":"La masse invariante d’un système est définie par son quadrivecteur énergie-impulsion total. Deux photons standards sans masse peuvent former un système de masse invariante positive.","test":"Mesurer énergie totale et quantité de mouvement vectorielle d’un système fermé ; distinguer invariant global et invariants individuels.","limitation":"Deux paquets sans masse d’énergie égale ; ne prouve pas que chaque entité possède une taille définie.","parameters":{"energy":"Énergie égale de chaque photon","angle":"Angle entre les quantités de mouvement"},"metrics":["Énergie au repos du système Mc²","Énergie totale","Masse invariante de chaque photon"],"series":["Énergie au repos du système Mc²"],"xLabel":"Angle (degrés)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/physical-existence/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2 and §5.2","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/physical-existence/","original_statement":"All that physically exists at any moment occupies area and has mass at that moment.","equation":"M²c⁴ = 2E₁E₂(1−cos θ)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"energy","label":"Equal photon energy","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"angle","label":"Angle between momenta","default":180,"min":0,"max":180,"step":1,"unit":"deg"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/physical-existence/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/physical-existence/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/physical-existence/"}},{"id":"spacetime","number":7,"title":"Géométrie et substance physique","statement":"L’espace-temps n’existe pas physiquement.","answer":"La géométrie relativiste est un cadre prédictif efficace. Rejeter la métaphore d’un tissu matériel ne réfute pas les temps propres, les trajectoires ou la gravité mesurés.","context":"La relativité restreinte donne le facteur inertiel √(1−β²). La relativité générale utilise une métrique dynamique. Le programme cherche des lois d’interaction reproduisant ces prédictions et offrant des tests discriminants.","test":"Comparer horloges de précision, orbites, ondes gravitationnelles et déviation de la lumière avec une loi alternative dérivée.","limitation":"Référence inertielle de relativité restreinte uniquement ; aucun calcul métrique gravitationnel ni théorie de remplacement.","parameters":{"beta":"Vitesse relative / c","duration":"Intervalle de référence"},"metrics":["Intervalle propre","Intervalle de référence","Rapport propre/référence"],"series":["Intervalle propre (s)","Intervalle de coordonnées (s)"],"xLabel":"Vitesse relative / c","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/spacetime/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/spacetime/","original_statement":"Spacetime does not physically exist.","equation":"Δτ/Δt = √(1−β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.8,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Reference interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/spacetime/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/spacetime/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/spacetime/"}},{"id":"fastest-signal","number":8,"title":"Vitesse de groupe et causalité","statement":"La lumière n’est pas ce qu’il y a de plus rapide dans l’univers.","answer":"Une caractéristique de champ peut être plus rapide qu’un paquet lent sans dépasser la limite causale locale. Aucun signal supraluminique contrôlable n’est démontré.","context":"Dans la relativité standard dans le vide, c est la vitesse causale locale. C-01 distingue plafond d’émission, front électromagnétique, caractéristiques causales, vitesse de groupe et taux de séparation.","test":"Mesurer les fronts de signal avec des extrémités synchronisées indépendamment et une théorie causale explicite.","limitation":"Trajets stationnaires normalisés ; le front est une référence stipulée, pas un nouveau champ plus rapide détecté.","parameters":{"fraction":"Vitesse du paquet / C","distance":"Longueur du trajet / C"},"metrics":["Arrivée du paquet","Arrivée de référence","Différence d’arrivée"],"series":["Temps d’arrivée du paquet","Temps d’arrivée de référence"],"xLabel":"Distance / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/fastest-signal/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3, C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/fastest-signal/","original_statement":"Light is not the fastest thing in the universe.","equation":"t_packet = D/v_g; t_front = D/C; Δt = D(1/v_g−1/C)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"fraction","label":"Packet speed / C","default":0.7,"min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"distance","label":"Path length / C","default":10,"min":0.1,"max":30,"step":0.1,"unit":"time units"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/fastest-signal/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/fastest-signal/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/fastest-signal/"}},{"id":"separation","number":9,"title":"Séparation des objets et vitesse locale","statement":"Les choses s’éloignent les unes des autres plus vite que la causalité ; le temps, l’espace ou l’espace-temps ne peuvent être étirés physiquement, car ce ne sont pas des substances physiques à étirer.","answer":"Un taux de séparation en coordonnées peut dépasser c sans qu’un corps ou un signal dépasse localement c.","context":"Pour des vitesses opposées égales βc dans un référentiel inertiel, la vitesse relative relativiste vaut 2βc/(1+β²). L’expérience de pensée C-01 des deux vaisseaux distingue séparation et propagation d’un signal.","test":"Mesurer l’arrivée des signaux et la vitesse relative avec les horloges et transformations de référentiel appropriées.","limitation":"Vitesses inertielles opposées et égales ; ni métrique cosmologique ni mécanisme de communication supraluminique.","parameters":{"beta":"Vitesse de chaque vaisseau / C"},"metrics":["Taux de séparation en coordonnées / C","Vitesse relative relativiste / C","Référence causale locale / C"],"series":["Séparation dans le référentiel commun / C","Vitesse relative relativiste / C","Référence locale"],"xLabel":"Vitesse de chaque vaisseau / C","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/separation/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3; C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/separation/","original_statement":"Things move apart from each other faster than causality; time, space, or spacetime cannot be physically stretched because they are not physical substances to stretch.","equation":"V_sep/C = 2β; v_rel/C = 2β/(1+β²)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"beta","label":"Each ship speed / C","default":0.9,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/separation/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/separation/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/separation/"}},{"id":"cosmic-size","number":10,"title":"Horizon et étendue du cosmos","statement":"La taille de l’univers est inconnue.","answer":"Une portée causale finie ne détermine pas l’étendue physique totale. L’horizon observable n’est pas nécessairement une paroi matérielle.","context":"Les horizons cosmologiques dépendent de l’expansion et se distinguent du rayon de Hubble. ArcSecs distingue le domaine observé d’un cosmos total hypothétique ; une frontière réelle exige un mécanisme et une signature propres.","test":"Confronter courbure, topologie, signatures du ciel et histoires causales indépendantes.","limitation":"Sphère euclidienne centrée et statique à portée imposée ; ne calcule pas l’univers observable réel.","parameters":{"reach":"Portée du signal","domain":"Rayon supposé du domaine"},"metrics":["Portée du signal","Rayon supposé du domaine","Fraction accessible"],"series":["Fraction de volume accessible"],"xLabel":"Rayon sphérique supposé","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-size/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and Chapters 30–32","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-size/","original_statement":"The size of the universe is unknown.","equation":"R_reach = C T; observed fraction = min(1,(R_reach/R_domain)³)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"reach","label":"Signal reach","default":4,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"domain","label":"Assumed domain radius","default":8,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-size/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-size/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-size/"}},{"id":"cosmic-age","number":11,"title":"Âge cosmologique et histoire totale","statement":"L’âge de l’univers est inconnu.","answer":"L’âge cosmologique conventionnel est inféré d’une histoire d’expansion calibrée. La durée de toute réalité physique hypothétique est une autre question.","context":"Une référence plate matière–Λ donne un âge à partir de H₀ et de la fraction de matière. ArcSecs conserve le Big Bang chaud comme histoire possible et distingue celle du domaine d’une éventuelle histoire antérieure.","test":"Comparer ajustements d’expansion, âges stellaires et autres chronomètres indépendants, avec leurs incertitudes.","limitation":"Référence plate matière–Λ sans rayonnement dans cette leçon analytique ; aucun ajustement de données ni inférence sur toute la réalité.","parameters":{"h0":"Constante de Hubble","matter":"Fraction de matière"},"metrics":["Âge du modèle","Fraction de matière","Fraction de Λ"],"series":["Âge du modèle (Gyr)"],"xLabel":"Constante de Hubble (km/s/Mpc)","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-age/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-age/","original_statement":"The age of the universe is unknown.","equation":"t₀ = 2 asinh(√((1−Ωm)/Ωm))/(3 H₀√(1−Ωm))","parameter_schema":[{"key":"h0","label":"Hubble constant","default":70,"min":45,"max":95,"step":1,"unit":"km/s/Mpc"},{"key":"matter","label":"Matter fraction","default":0.3,"min":0.05,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/cosmic-age/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/cosmic-age/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/cosmic-age/"}},{"id":"distance-inference","number":12,"title":"Distances et tailles astronomiques","statement":"Les objets de l’univers sont plus petits et plus proches qu’ils ne le paraissent.","answer":"Les corrections de distance peuvent être de l’un ou l’autre signe. La prémisse « plus petits et plus proches » n’est pas une correction universelle établie.","context":"Ancrages géométriques, luminosités calibrées, règles, spectres et intervalles temporels apportent des contraintes complémentaires. Ce contrôle isole un biais de magnitude par rapport à une distance calibrée.","test":"Confronter ancrages géométriques, extinction, flux, angles, spectres et intervalles avec des paramètres communs et des données réservées.","limitation":"Biais de magnitude signé ; les valeurs négatives décrivent un biais de calibration ou d’éclat, pas une extinction physique négative par la poussière. Aucun ajustement astronomique.","parameters":{"actual":"Distance calibrée","extinction":"Atténuation non modélisée"},"metrics":["Distance inférée","Distance calibrée","Taille inférée/calibrée"],"series":["Distance inférée (Mpc)","Distance calibrée (Mpc)"],"xLabel":"Biais de magnitude signé","englishUrl":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/distance-inference/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and distance audit","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"},"url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/distance-inference/","original_statement":"Objects in the universe are smaller and closer than they appear.","equation":"D_inferred/D = 10^(A/5); size ratio = distance ratio (fixed small angle)","parameter_schema":[{"key":"actual","label":"Calibrated distance","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"Mpc"},{"key":"extinction","label":"Unmodeled attenuation","default":0.5,"min":-1,"max":3,"step":0.05,"unit":"mag"}],"alternates":{"en":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/en/distance-inference/","es":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/es/distance-inference/","fr":"https://beyondspacetime.org/global/fr/distance-inference/"}}],"glossary":[{"term":"Masse invariante","definition":"Masse définie par l’invariant énergie-impulsion total, et non simplement par l’énergie divisée par c².","lesson":"photon-mass"},{"term":"Vitesse de groupe","definition":"Vitesse de propagation de l’enveloppe d’un paquet d’ondes pour une dispersion donnée. Distincte de la vitesse du front.","lesson":"light-slowing"},{"term":"Temps propre","definition":"Temps mesuré par une horloge idéale le long de sa ligne d’univers en relativité.","lesson":"constant-time"},{"term":"Progression universelle T","definition":"Paramètre commun d’évolution proposé ; pas une horloge absolue établie expérimentalement.","lesson":"when"},{"term":"Front causal","definition":"Limite avant d’une perturbation causale dans une théorie donnée, distincte en général de la vitesse de groupe.","lesson":"fastest-signal"},{"term":"Taux de séparation","definition":"Variation de distance par unité de temps selon une convention de coordonnées ; pas nécessairement une vitesse relative locale.","lesson":"separation"},{"term":"Horizon observable","definition":"Limite d’accessibilité causale déterminée par l’histoire cosmique supposée, pas nécessairement une paroi physique.","lesson":"cosmic-size"},{"term":"Constante de Hubble","definition":"Paramètre du taux d’expansion actuel dans un modèle cosmologique conventionnel.","lesson":"cosmic-age"},{"term":"Biais de distance","definition":"Différence entre une distance inférée et une distance calibrée sous des hypothèses déclarées. Peut être de signe positif ou négatif.","lesson":"distance-inference"},{"term":"Contrôle négatif","definition":"Condition spécifiée du modèle censée supprimer un effet ou révéler un échec ; la réussir ne constitue pas une validation empirique.","lesson":"spacetime"}]}},"foundations":{"photon-mass":{"slug":"photon-mass","title":"Light, energy and invariant mass","answer":"Light has measurable energy and momentum. A nonzero invariant photon rest mass is a separate hypothesis, not a consequence of radiation pressure or gravity.","explorer":"A mirror moves when it receives momentum from light. This is a direct physical interaction even when the light has zero invariant mass. Think of a gap in the energy–momentum relation as a proposed extra property, not a new name for the energy already carried by light.","standard":"Maxwell electrodynamics and the standard photon describe zero invariant mass. A positive invariant mass would change the dispersion relation and can introduce additional polarization and electrostatic screening constraints.","candidate":"Review 6.0 §5.2 treats a positive frequency gap in a candidate sector. Here C and initial energy are normalized to one, so a gap fraction g yields group speed √(1−g²). A photon carrying energy is not proof of a positive gap.","equation":"E² = C²p² + Eₘ²;  v_g/C = √(1−g²),  g = Eₘ/E","params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.2,"min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"series":["Group speed / C","Momentum C / E"],"xLabel":"Rest-energy fraction","control":{"gap":0},"challenge":"Choose g=0. The speed and normalized momentum both return one.","observation":"Compare dispersion, static electric forces, polarization and emission/receiver spectra using shared parameters. A bound on rest mass is not a detection.","limitation":"Single fixed-energy dispersion control. Does not derive the gap, its origin or an astronomical drag law.","quiz":"Does transferring momentum establish invariant photon mass? No; the massless reference transfers momentum too.","topic":"photon-mass","lab":"photon-aging","reference":"Review 6.0 §5.2; book Chapter 30L","number":1,"statement":"Photons have mass.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/photon-mass/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.2; book Chapter 30L","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"constant-time":{"slug":"constant-time","title":"One progression, different clock readings","answer":"The proposed universal progression T is a research choice. Different measured clock counts must be explained; identical progression does not establish a detectable absolute clock.","explorer":"Two clocks can accumulate different numbers of ticks during the same specified coordinate interval. A change in a physical clock rate is an observable; interpreting that change requires a model of the clock and the comparison protocol.","standard":"Special relativity predicts proper time along an inertial trajectory. Atomic clocks, particle lifetimes and gravitational time comparisons constrain any alternative account.","candidate":"ArcSecs uses T and a process rate ν(T); a candidate must derive each physical rate and reproduce the complete set of comparisons, not just choose a rate that fits one clock.","equation":"N_A = ν_A T; N_B = ν_B T; N_SR = ν_A T√(1−β²)","params":[{"key":"rate","label":"Reference rate","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":5,"step":0.1,"unit":"Hz"},{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.6,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Coordinate interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"ratio","label":"Candidate B/A rate","default":0.8,"min":0.1,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""}],"series":["Reference counts","Candidate counts","SR comparator counts"],"xLabel":"Coordinate interval (s)","control":{"beta":0,"ratio":1},"challenge":"At β=0 and equal rates all three traces coincide.","observation":"Compare clock technologies and moving-particle lifetimes using the same specified physical rate law.","limitation":"Constant stipulated rates; the SR comparator is an inertial control, not a derivation of preferred time.","quiz":"Can a fitted clock-rate ratio by itself distinguish two theories? No; an independently predicted difference is needed.","topic":"clocks","lab":"clocks","reference":"Review 6.0 §5.3; book Chapter 25","number":2,"statement":"Time is constant.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/constant-time/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §5.3; book Chapter 25","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"light-slowing":{"slug":"light-slowing","title":"Distinguishing energy loss from slowing","answer":"Energy, frequency and group speed are distinct. A fixed-gap drag model permits energy loss with very little speed change, and the zero-gap limit does not slow at all.","explorer":"Imagine comparing two light packets at emission and reception. A spectral shift measures a frequency ratio relative to standards. A travel-time experiment measures a different quantity. Both must be predicted before claiming that a packet slowed.","standard":"Redshifts arise in standard physics from relative motion, gravitational conditions and cosmological evolution. Propagation in media can alter group speed; a spectral shift alone does not identify a mechanism.","candidate":"The fixed-gap momentum-loss control in the book is an explicitly phenomenological branch. Review 6.0 also studies conversion and energy transfer; a stationary loss law alone does not generate observed arrival stretching.","equation":"p = p₀ exp(−γT); E = √(p²+g²); v_g = p/E","params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rest-energy fraction","default":0.1,"min":0,"max":0.9,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"drag","label":"Momentum-loss rate","default":0.3,"min":0,"max":2,"step":0.05,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Progression interval","default":5,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"series":["Energy / initial","Group speed / C","Transferred energy"],"xLabel":"Progression T","control":{"gap":0,"drag":0.5},"challenge":"Set gap to zero and positive drag. Energy falls, but speed stays one.","observation":"Identify the energy recipient and test pulse stretching, image blur, spectral noise and independent timing.","limitation":"Normalized fixed C, initial E=1, drag imposed rather than derived. No microscopic bath or universal cosmology.","quiz":"Does a red spectrum prove that the received photon moved more slowly? No.","topic":"light-history","lab":"photon-aging","reference":"Review 6.0 Parts XXXIII–XXXIV; book Chapter 30R","number":3,"statement":"Light slows down over time.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/light-slowing/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 Parts XXXIII–XXXIV; book Chapter 30R","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"when":{"slug":"when","title":"Time as event ordering","answer":"The charter treats When as a relation between events. That ontological choice must be distinguished from measured duration and relativistic proper time.","explorer":"A source emits repeated pulses. A receiver records their arrival intervals. If every pulse takes the same travel time, all arrivals are shifted by that delay but the intervals remain unchanged.","standard":"In relativity, proper time is measurable along a worldline; coordinate times describe events in chosen frames. Neither requires a flowing material.","candidate":"The physical-first account models ordered source events, transport and receiver counts separately. A variable transit delay can change arrival intervals, but an identical delay cannot.","equation":"t_emit(n)=nP; t_arr(n)=nP+L/C; Δt_arr=P","params":[{"key":"period","label":"Emission interval","default":2,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"},{"key":"delay","label":"Fixed transit delay","default":5,"min":0,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"s"}],"series":["Emission time","Arrival time"],"xLabel":"Pulse index","control":{"delay":0},"challenge":"Increase the fixed delay. The two lines remain parallel.","observation":"Measure a series of pulses with calibrated endpoint clocks; vary transit conditions independently.","limitation":"Stationary endpoints, constant transit delay, identical clock units; not a synchronization theory or a full redshift model.","quiz":"Does a five-second fixed transit delay stretch a two-second pulse interval? No; it shifts all arrivals equally.","topic":"clocks","lab":"clocks","reference":"Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","number":4,"statement":"Time does not physically exist; it is the “When.”","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/when/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"where":{"slug":"where","title":"Space as measurable relations","answer":"Relative position, separation and geometry are measurable. The choice not to call space a material does not remove geometric laws.","explorer":"Move a coordinate origin on a map: two objects keep the same physical separation even though both coordinates change. In a model, specify what is invariant under a coordinate change.","standard":"Euclidean geometry is a useful local approximation; relativistic geometry predicts measured trajectories and timing in its domain. Coordinates themselves are not material objects.","candidate":"ArcSecs treats Where as relative arrangement. Physical states and interactions must give coordinate-independent predictions for actual rulers and other instruments.","equation":"d = √((x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²)","params":[{"key":"dx","label":"Horizontal separation","default":3,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"dy","label":"Vertical separation","default":4,"min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""},{"key":"shift","label":"Common origin offset","default":2,"min":-10,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":""}],"series":["Object A x","Object B x","Separation"],"xLabel":"Common offset","control":{"shift":0},"challenge":"Change the origin offset. The distance remains √(3²+4²)=5 at the defaults.","observation":"Repeat ranging with different reference origins and compare invariant separations in the declared measurement regime.","limitation":"Flat two-dimensional geometry; not a model of curved geometry, cosmological distance or absolute coordinates.","quiz":"Does changing the origin change the measured distance between two fixed objects? No.","topic":"physical-first","lab":"parallax","reference":"Review 6.0 §2.1; book foundations interpretation","number":5,"statement":"Space does not physically exist; it is the “Where.”","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/where/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1; book foundations interpretation","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"physical-existence":{"slug":"physical-existence","title":"Physical attributes and system mass","answer":"Physical states carry measurable energy and momentum, but energy-equivalent mass, invariant mass and geometric extent are distinct quantities.","explorer":"Two beams of light can each carry energy and momentum. Their combined system can have nonzero invariant mass if they travel in opposite directions, even if neither individual photon has invariant mass.","standard":"For a system, invariant mass is defined by total four-momentum. A field state need not have a rigid classical boundary or nonzero individual rest mass.","candidate":"The charter asks for a physical inventory. Its technical interpretation requires defining mass and extent operationally before proposing an invariant mass for every entity. This control uses the standard massless two-photon reference.","equation":"M²c⁴ = 2E₁E₂(1−cos θ)","params":[{"key":"energy","label":"Equal photon energy","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"angle","label":"Angle between momenta","default":180,"min":0,"max":180,"step":1,"unit":"deg"}],"series":["System rest energy Mc²"],"xLabel":"Angle (degrees)","control":{"angle":0},"challenge":"Compare parallel beams with opposing beams. Parallel beams give zero system invariant mass.","observation":"Measure total energy and vector momentum of a closed system; distinguish the aggregate invariant from constituent invariant masses.","limitation":"Two massless packets of equal energy; no localization measurement or proof that every entity has a sharp size.","quiz":"Do two individually massless photons necessarily form a massless system? No, the result depends on their total momentum.","topic":"physical-first","lab":"photon-aging","reference":"Review 6.0 §2 and §5.2","number":6,"statement":"All that physically exists at any moment occupies area and has mass at that moment.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/physical-existence/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2 and §5.2","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"spacetime":{"slug":"spacetime","title":"Geometry versus a literal fabric","answer":"Relativistic geometry is a successful predictive framework. Rejecting a literal fabric metaphor does not reject measured proper times, trajectories or gravity.","explorer":"Two ideal inertial clock rates can be compared in a chosen reference frame. A calculation can describe the measured ratio without treating time as a material flowing at a different speed.","standard":"The standard inertial proper-time factor is √(1−β²). General relativity extends the predictions to gravity using a dynamical metric.","candidate":"The research seeks physical interaction laws that recover the same predictions and offer independently discriminating tests. The standard factor here is a comparator, not a proposed alternative mechanism.","equation":"Δτ/Δt = √(1−β²)","params":[{"key":"beta","label":"Relative speed / c","default":0.8,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"duration","label":"Reference interval","default":10,"min":1,"max":60,"step":1,"unit":"s"}],"series":["Proper interval (s)","Coordinate interval (s)"],"xLabel":"Relative speed / c","control":{"beta":0},"challenge":"At rest, proper and reference intervals agree; at β=0.8 the ratio is 0.6.","observation":"Compare precision clock measurements, orbital effects, gravitational waves and light deflection with a derived alternative law.","limitation":"Inertial special-relativity reference only. No gravitational metric calculation or replacement theory is supplied.","quiz":"Does successful geometry imply a rubber-like spacetime material? No; the model predicts measurements, not a literal sheet.","topic":"gravity","lab":"clocks","reference":"Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","number":7,"statement":"Spacetime does not physically exist.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/spacetime/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2.1 and §5.3","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"fastest-signal":{"slug":"fastest-signal","title":"Group speed and causal speed","answer":"A field characteristic may be faster than a slow massive packet without exceeding the local causal maximum. No controllable faster-than-light signal is demonstrated.","explorer":"Compare a slower packet with a reference front launched from the same point. The front can arrive first without either crossing the specified maximum speed.","standard":"In standard vacuum relativity c is the local causal speed. Group speed and front speed in dispersive media are distinct. A closing rate is not a local information speed.","candidate":"C-01 separates the emission ceiling, electromagnetic front, all-sector causal characteristic, packet group speed and separation rate. The research requires a derived ordering and physical signal protocol.","equation":"t_packet = D/v_g; t_front = D/C; Δt = D(1/v_g−1/C)","params":[{"key":"fraction","label":"Packet speed / C","default":0.7,"min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.01,"unit":""},{"key":"distance","label":"Path length / C","default":10,"min":0.1,"max":30,"step":0.1,"unit":"time units"}],"series":["Packet arrival time","Reference arrival time"],"xLabel":"Distance / C","control":{"fraction":1},"challenge":"Set packet speed equal to the reference front; the arrival difference vanishes.","observation":"Test actual signal fronts with independently synchronized endpoints and an explicitly stated causal theory.","limitation":"Normalized stationary paths; the front is a specified reference, not an experimentally detected new faster field.","quiz":"Does a front overtaking a slower packet prove faster-than-causal communication? No.","topic":"gravity","lab":"shell","reference":"Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3, C-01","number":8,"statement":"Light is not the fastest thing in the universe.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/fastest-signal/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3, C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"separation":{"slug":"separation","title":"Closing and separation rates versus causality","answer":"Coordinate separation rates may exceed a local light-speed benchmark; this does not imply that one body or signal travels locally faster than c.","explorer":"In a common reference frame two ships move oppositely at 0.9c. Their separation changes at 1.8c. An observer on either ship measures the other using relativistic velocity addition, not simple subtraction in the common frame.","standard":"Special relativity gives relative speed 2β/(1+β²) times c for opposite equal speeds in one inertial frame. Cosmological recession is a distinct large-scale quantity.","candidate":"Review 6.0 C-01 uses this two-spacecraft thought experiment to separate inherited motion and emission. It expressly does not demonstrate single-photon superluminal motion.","equation":"V_sep/C = 2β; v_rel/C = 2β/(1+β²)","params":[{"key":"beta","label":"Each ship speed / C","default":0.9,"min":0,"max":0.99,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"series":["Common-frame separation rate / C","SR measured relative speed / C","Local benchmark"],"xLabel":"Each ship speed / C","control":{"beta":0},"challenge":"Increase β past 0.5. Separation exceeds one while measured relative speed remains below one.","observation":"Measure signal arrival and relative velocity with the appropriate clocks and frame transformations.","limitation":"Equal opposite inertial velocities, not a cosmological metric or a superluminal communication mechanism.","quiz":"Is a 1.8c closing rate a measurement of one signal moving at 1.8c? No.","topic":"gravity","lab":"recession","reference":"Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3; C-01","number":9,"statement":"Things move apart from each other faster than causality; time, space, or spacetime cannot be physically stretched because they are not physical substances to stretch.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/separation/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §3.1–3.3; C-01","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"cosmic-size":{"slug":"cosmic-size","title":"Observable reach and total cosmic extent","answer":"A finite causal reach does not determine the total physical extent of the universe. The observable horizon is not necessarily a material wall.","explorer":"In a toy stationary world, a signal traveling at constant speed can reach a finite radius in a finite time. A hypothetical boundary beyond that radius is not detectable by this particular experiment.","standard":"Cosmological particle horizons depend on the expansion history and differ from Hubble radius and event horizon. Observations constrain curvature and accessible structure, not every possible parent domain.","candidate":"ArcSecs considers the total cosmos separately from the observed domain. A real boundary or outside region would require a field/interface model and observational signature.","equation":"R_reach = C T; observed fraction = min(1,(R_reach/R_domain)³)","params":[{"key":"reach","label":"Signal reach","default":4,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"},{"key":"domain","label":"Assumed domain radius","default":8,"min":0.1,"max":20,"step":0.1,"unit":"normalized"}],"series":["Accessible volume fraction"],"xLabel":"Assumed spherical radius","control":{"domain":4},"challenge":"Increase the assumed domain radius at fixed reach. The accessible fraction decreases without changing the reach.","observation":"Use independent observations of curvature, topology, sky signatures and causal histories; a domain boundary needs separate evidence.","limitation":"Stationary Euclidean centered sphere with prescribed reach; not a calculation of the actual observable universe or its size.","quiz":"Does seeing only a finite region establish a physical edge? No.","topic":"bubble-universes","lab":"bubble","reference":"Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and Chapters 30–32","number":10,"statement":"The size of the universe is unknown.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-size/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and Chapters 30–32","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"cosmic-age":{"slug":"cosmic-age","title":"Modeled cosmic age and total history","answer":"The conventional cosmic age is an inference from a calibrated expansion history. The duration of all hypothetical physical reality is a different, unanswered question.","explorer":"The time since a specified early phase can be estimated from the rate of evolution. An unobserved earlier phase is not automatically ruled out by that calculation, nor is it established by proposing it.","standard":"A flat matter–Λ reference gives a calculable expansion age for specified H₀ and matter fraction. The full standard inference includes radiation, observational constraints and uncertainties.","candidate":"The research distinguishes an age inferred for our domain from an unknown parent history. It retains the hot Big Bang as a possible physical history and requires independent chronometers.","equation":"t₀ = 2 asinh(√((1−Ωm)/Ωm))/(3 H₀√(1−Ωm))","params":[{"key":"h0","label":"Hubble constant","default":70,"min":45,"max":95,"step":1,"unit":"km/s/Mpc"},{"key":"matter","label":"Matter fraction","default":0.3,"min":0.05,"max":0.95,"step":0.01,"unit":""}],"series":["Model age (Gyr)"],"xLabel":"Hubble constant (km/s/Mpc)","control":{"h0":67.4,"matter":0.315},"challenge":"Change H₀ while holding Ωm fixed. The model age scales inversely with H₀.","observation":"Compare cosmic expansion fits with stellar and other physical age indicators, using the same model assumptions and uncertainties.","limitation":"Flat matter–Λ reference, radiation omitted for this analytic lesson; no data fit or inference about all reality.","quiz":"Does this model age determine the age of a hypothetical parent cosmos? No.","topic":"cosmic-evolution","lab":"big-bang","reference":"Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26","number":11,"statement":"The age of the universe is unknown.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/cosmic-age/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}},"distance-inference":{"slug":"distance-inference","title":"When astronomical distances and sizes can change","answer":"Distance corrections are testable and can have either sign. The charter’s smaller-and-closer premise must not become an assumed correction for every object.","explorer":"A source dimmed by dust may appear farther away if the dust is ignored. A separate geometric parallax measurement can test that inference. At fixed small angular extent the inferred physical size scales with distance.","standard":"Geometric anchors, calibrated candles, rulers, spectroscopy and time intervals give complementary constraints. A reclassified foreground object does not invalidate the distances of spectroscopically confirmed galaxies.","candidate":"ArcSecs calls for geometric-first audits and joint fits, with positive and negative controls. This lesson isolates an unmodeled extinction term and a calibrated distance.","equation":"D_inferred/D = 10^(A/5); size ratio = distance ratio (fixed small angle)","params":[{"key":"actual","label":"Calibrated distance","default":1,"min":0.1,"max":10,"step":0.1,"unit":"Mpc"},{"key":"extinction","label":"Unmodeled attenuation","default":0.5,"min":-1,"max":3,"step":0.05,"unit":"mag"}],"series":["Inferred distance (Mpc)","Calibrated distance (Mpc)"],"xLabel":"Signed magnitude bias","control":{"extinction":0},"challenge":"Set the bias to zero for equality; negative signed calibration bias gives the opposite direction.","observation":"Compare geometric anchors, extinction, flux, angles, spectra and arrival intervals with shared parameters and held-out observations.","limitation":"Signed magnitude offset is a sensitivity control; negative values model calibration/brightening bias, not physical negative dust extinction. No universal correction or astronomical fit.","quiz":"Does one smaller-and-closer reclassification prove every distance is too large? No.","topic":"distances","lab":"distance","reference":"Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and distance audit","number":12,"statement":"Objects in the universe are smaller and closer than they appear.","epistemic_status":"charter_premise","model_status":"conditional_educational_control","url":"https://beyondspacetime.org/foundations/distance-inference/","source":{"title":"ArcSecs Scientific Review 6.0","section":"§2, printed pp. 6–7; Review 6.0 §2; book Chapter 26 and distance audit","url":"https://arcsecs.com/read-scientific-paper/"}}},"source_sha256":"9d83062c871056f65269b268567502ccea4c9c56502a77a667da475945f0681b"};
